I found the main CAP/DA coverage sections and checked for amendments. The current authoritative document is Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, effective Sept. 1, 2025, and the tool returned no amendments. I’m answering from the general coverage criteria in section 3.1 and the CAP/DA-specific eligibility criteria in section 3.2.1.## General eligibility criteria for CAP/DA under NC Medicaid

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)**, a person generally must meet both the **general Medicaid coverage criteria** and the **CAP/DA-specific criteria**. The policy returned by the amendment check is the current authoritative document, **effective September 1, 2025**.  
Citations: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 1) General Medicaid coverage criteria
The service must be:

- **Individualized and specific** to the person’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and **not more than the person needs**;
- Able to be **safely furnished**; and
- Not available through an **equally effective, more conservative, or less costly** statewide alternative;
- Not furnished mainly for the **convenience** of the beneficiary, caregiver, or provider.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.1.

### 2) CAP/DA-specific eligibility criteria
For CAP/DA, Medicaid covers services when the beneficiary:

- Meets the required **HCBS nursing facility level of care (LOC)** as determined by NC Medicaid;
- Needs long-term care support at a level typically provided in an institution, tied to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
- Has a completed **CAP assessment** showing a **reasonable indication** that the person will need at least one CAP/DA home- and community-based service within **30 calendar days** of the evaluation;
- Has a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis to prevent institutionalization;
- Or has a signed service plan authorizing short-term **equipment, modification, or technology** needed to return to or remain in the primary private residence and prevent institutional placement;
- Can maintain **health, safety, and well-being** in the primary private residence or another approved location with formal and informal supports;
- Has paid care needs that can be maintained **within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- Has a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- Has an **emergency back-up and disaster plan** identifying who will provide care if regular caregivers are unavailable or during a public health emergency.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1.

### 3) Extra criteria for consumer-directed services
If the person wants **consumer-directed CAP/DA services**, the policy adds requirements, including that the beneficiary (or representative):

- Understands the rights and responsibilities of directing their own care;
- Is willing and emotionally capable of taking on employer-type responsibilities, or has a representative who can do so;
- Has an approved service plan with the amount, duration, frequency, and NPI of services;
- Receives services consistent with policy and applicable laws; and
- Signs and follows the beneficiary rights and responsibilities.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1.

## Bottom line
In plain terms, a person generally qualifies for CAP/DA if they:

1. Need a **nursing-facility level of care**,  
2. Can be safely served at home or another approved setting,  
3. Have a documented need for CAP/DA waiver services shown through assessment and service planning,  
4. Can be served **cost-effectively** within waiver limits, and  
5. Have the necessary medical coordination and emergency backup supports in place.  

