I found the current CAP/DA policy document and confirmed through the amendment tool that the authoritative document is the current September 1, 2025 version of 3K-2, with no separate amendments returned. I’ve also pulled the core sections on coverage criteria, equipment/home modification requirements, and documentation. Next I’ll answer from those retrieved sections and distinguish the actual home-modification requirements from the MDT documentation requirements.## CAP/DA home modifications: requirements and MDT documentation

Under current **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)**, home modifications are covered only in specified circumstances and with specified documentation. The policy effective date shown in the retrieved document is **September 1, 2025**, and the amendment check returned the same document as the authoritative current version. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 1) Basic coverage requirements for home modifications

A beneficiary must have a **signed service plan** authorizing the installation of equipment, modification, or technology **on a short-term basis** in order to **return to or remain in the primary private residence and prevent institutional placement**. The installation of the equipment or modification **must be completed within three calendar months of service plan approval**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy section on equipment/modification/technology states that home modifications may be made to:

- a **primary private residence** owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a **rented residence when the modifications are portable**; or
- a **rented residence when portable modifications are allowed by the owner**.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 2) Types of home modifications the policy lists

The retrieved CAP/DA policy text lists examples of covered home-related modifications, including:

- ramps;
- grab bars;
- safety rails and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair/walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom accessibility modifications, including toilet, shower, tub, sink fixtures, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- bedroom modifications for hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications, including cabinets, sink fixtures/modifications, faucet controls, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- door handle replacements;
- door modifications;
- hydraulic/manual/electronic lifts used primarily inside the home;
- non-skid surfaces;
- certain smart-home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- portable or whole-house air filtration systems in limited medical circumstances described by policy;
- portable back-up generator for a ventilator in the limited circumstances described by policy; and
- environmental control units/electronic aids to daily living.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The same section also includes costs associated with the modification, such as:

- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase;
- necessary permits and inspections;
- taxes and delivery charges;
- installation;
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and applicable inspectors to verify safety and fit with the beneficiary’s needs; and
- repairs, so long as repair cost does not exceed replacement cost and the item is not covered by warranty.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 3) Important limits/exclusions

The CAP/DA policy also says funding cannot be used for certain items, including:

- items **not of direct medical or remedial benefit** to the beneficiary;
- items covered under the **Home Health Final Rule**;
- items covered through **Medicaid State Plan DME, orthotics, prosthetics, and home health supplies**;
- items that are **recreational**;
- items of **general utility to a non-disabled person**;
- service agreements and maintenance contracts not related to the approved service;
- warranties;
- certain pool/spa/sauna-related equipment not approved in the person-centered service plan or not medically necessary; and
- certain technology or monitoring items not approved in the service plan.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 4) What the Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) must provide

The clearest direct MDT requirement in the retrieved policy text is in **Section 5.9.1**. It requires:

- **an MDT recommendation** identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the requested assistive technology, equipment, or **home modification**;
- the recommendation must **state the cost** of the item required;
- **a training plan** for the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment, with training documented by the case manager as completed and signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- a **statement of need** identified in the service plan;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- any **other information required** for the specific item requested;
- when quotes are required, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**; and
- in special circumstances, **NC Medicaid or its designee determines the appropriate professional(s)** who must make written recommendations.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 5) Additional documentation the policy requires for the request

The retrieved policy text also requires the following documentation for equipment/supply/adaptation/modification requests:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life** of the equipment and the **length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit** from it; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the item/modification was provided and the cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Short practical summary

For a **home modification under CAP/DA**, the policy requires that the modification be:

- tied to a **signed service plan**;
- needed to help the person **remain in or return to the home and avoid institutionalization**;
- completed within **3 calendar months** of service-plan approval; and
- for an approved residence type, with the modification fitting within CAP/DA’s covered categories and not falling into listed exclusions.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

For **MDT documentation**, the file should include at minimum:

- the **MDT recommendation** describing the need for the home modification;
- the **cost**;
- the **service-plan statement of need**;
- **medical-need support** from the MDT or physician, when applicable;
- a **training plan** and training completion documentation if relevant;
- any required **quotes**;
- the **Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment**;
- **physician order**, if applicable;
- **appropriate professional recommendation**, if required;
- estimated useful life / expected benefit duration; and
- supplier **invoice** with date and cost.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

