NC Medicaid allows **some speech and language evaluation and treatment interventions** to be delivered by **telehealth**, but only under the conditions in both the speech therapy policy and the general telehealth policy.

### Speech-therapy-specific conditions
Under **Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, a **select set** of speech and language evaluation and treatment interventions may be provided by telehealth **as described in Clinical Coverage Policy 1-H**. The policy says telehealth delivery **may be medically necessary** when:

- the beneficiary’s **medical condition is such that exposure to others should be avoided**, or
- the beneficiary’s **location is remote or underserved**, so access to appropriately qualified providers is limited.

Policy 10A also requires that:

- **unless in-person care is contraindicated or unavailable, telehealth must be used as an adjunct to in-person care and not as a replacement**;
- telehealth must be used **in the beneficiary’s best interest**, **not for the therapist’s convenience**; and
- telehealth must **never be used solely to increase therapist productivity**.  
  ([Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment), effective 2025-02-01, current; amendment check performed)

### General telehealth conditions that also apply
Because Policy 10A expressly points to **Clinical Coverage Policy 1-H**, the general telehealth requirements there also apply. Under Policy 1-H, providers must ensure that:

- the service can be **safely and effectively delivered** by telehealth;
- the beneficiary’s **behavioral, physical, and cognitive abilities** are considered;
- the beneficiary’s **safety** is appropriate for the complexity of the service;
- if a caregiver/facilitator is needed, that person’s **ability and safety** are also considered;
- the service complies with **professional standards of care**, scope-of-practice rules, and other federal/state/institutional requirements;
- the provider obtains and documents **verbal or written consent** (or documents why consent could not be obtained in extenuating circumstances);
- the beneficiary is **not required** to use telehealth and must be allowed **in-person services if requested**;
- the provider verifies the beneficiary’s identity using **two identifiers** before starting the telehealth service; and
- the provider protects **privacy and confidentiality** to the best of their ability.  
  ([Clinical Coverage Policy 1-H](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1h-telehealth-virtual-communications-and-remote-patient-monitoring/download?attachment), §§ 3.1, 3.2.1, effective 2025-02-01, current; amendment check performed)

### Bottom line
Speech therapy can be provided via telehealth under NC Medicaid when:

1. it is one of the **speech/language services allowed under Policy 10A and Policy 1-H**;
2. telehealth is **medically necessary**, such as to avoid exposure or because the beneficiary is in a remote/underserved area with limited access;
3. the service can be **safely and effectively** delivered remotely;
4. telehealth is used **for the beneficiary’s benefit**, not provider convenience or productivity; and
5. telehealth is generally **supplemental to in-person care**, unless in-person care is **contraindicated or unavailable**.

## Important limitation
I could verify that Policy 10A authorizes only a **“select set”** of speech/language evaluations and treatments by telehealth, but the specific list of which CPT/service codes are included was **not visible in the retrieved text** from the corpus sections I opened. So I can confirm the governing conditions above, but I **could not verify from the retrieved text exactly which speech therapy codes or interventions are telehealth-eligible**.

